Exercise: Calculate the difference between current row and previous row's sales amount by product.

Solution: We used an OUTER query and an INNER query.
The INNER query uses an INNER JOIN to connect the SalesOrderHeader and SalesOrderDetail tables, and an INNER JOIN to connect those to the Product table.

The INNER query calculates the total sales by product name and product ID using the SUM function, while the OUTER query calculates the sales amount between the product of the current row and the previous row using the LAG function.


Tables used
[AdventureWorks2022].[Sales].[SalesOrderHeader]
[AdventureWorks2022].[Sales].[SalesOrderDetail]
[AdventureWorks2022].[Production].[Product]

Functions used
SUM function
LAG() OVER
ROUND function
FORMAT function


T-SQL code
USE AdventureWorks2022;
GO

SELECT	X.ProductName
	,FORMAT(ROUND(X.SalesByProductName, 2, 2), '#,#.##')  AS SalesByProductName
	,FORMAT(ROUND(X.SalesByProductName - LAG(X.SalesByProductName) OVER (ORDER BY X.SalesByProductName), 2, 2), '#,#.##') AS SalesDiff
FROM 
(
SELECT	Product.[Name] AS ProductName
	,Product.[ProductID] AS ProductID
	,SUM(SalesOrderDetail.[LineTotal]) AS SalesByProductName
FROM [AdventureWorks2022].[Sales].[SalesOrderHeader] AS SalesOrderHeader
INNER JOIN [AdventureWorks2022].[Sales].[SalesOrderDetail] AS SalesOrderDetail
ON SalesOrderHeader.[SalesOrderID] = SalesOrderDetail.[SalesOrderID]
INNER JOIN [AdventureWorks2022].[Production].[Product] AS Product
ON SalesOrderDetail.[ProductID] = Product.[ProductID]
GROUP BY Product.[Name], Product.[ProductID]
) AS X
ORDER BY X.SalesByProductName ASC


